 ## Overview
-3D Texture Storage maps a 3D voxel grid into a hardware-accelerated 3D texture resource, leveraging native texture sampling units for coordinate indexing [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of inefficient memory access patterns in ray traversal by storing spatially adjacent voxels contiguously in memory, thereby improving cache locality compared to linear byte buffers [S1, unofficial]. It differs from raw buffer storage by delegating 3D coordinate indexing to the texture hardware, though it introduces specific pitfalls regarding coordinate rounding during sampling [S2, unofficial].
+3D Texture Storage maps a volumetric voxel grid into a hardware-accelerated 3D texture resource, leveraging native 3D coordinate indexing and texture caching for efficient sampling during ray traversal [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike linear byte buffers which require manual address calculation, this method allows the GPU to handle spatial locality automatically [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Voxel Texture Encoding](8970-voxel-texture-encoding.md), which embeds 3D data into 2D textures to enable specific pipeline techniques like ping-pong recursion [8970-voxel-texture-encoding.md].
 
